"Play middle C on a piano, and then play the same note on a violin. The pitch is the same. The loudness can be the same too. Yet you would never mix them up, even with your eyes closed. Something about the sound is different. Musicians call it tone color, or timbre (say \"TAM-ber\").": "9d1dbd3d931a",
"Play middle C on a piano, and then play the same note on a violin. The pitch is the same. The loudness can be the same too. Yet you would never mix them up, even with your eyes closed. Something about the sound is different. Musicians call it tone color, or timbre (say \"TAM-ber\"). A well-known standard from the American National Standards Institute describes timbre as the quality that lets a listener tell that two sounds are different, even when they have the same loudness and the same pitch. So timbre is not pitch, and it is not loudness. It is the third thing. To see where timbre comes from, look inside one note. A string does not shake in only one way. It shakes as a whole, and at the same time in two halves, in three thirds, and so on. Each way of shaking is called a harmonic. The whole-string shake is the fundamental, or the first harmonic. The second harmonic has exactly twice the frequency, and the third has three times, and so on. The second harmonic is also called the first overtone. You do not hear these harmonics as separate notes. You hear one note, and its pitch is the fundamental. But the mix matters. If the harmonics are strong, the sound is bright and buzzy. If only a few are present, the sound is round and soft. The mix of harmonics changes the shape of the sound wave, even though the repeating rate, and so the pitch, stays the same. Where does the mix come from? Partly from how you start the string. Helmholtz, a German scientist who wrote a famous book on sound in 1863, noticed that a string struck in its middle loses its second partial tone. In the model you can check this: pluck exactly in the middle and every even harmonic disappears, because a node sits right where you pluck. Pluck near the end and many high harmonics are strong. Partly it comes from the instrument itself. A clarinet, for example, mostly makes the odd harmonics. Time matters too. A piano string is struck by a hammer, so the note starts at once and then fades. A bowed violin string can start more gently and keep going. Reference works on timbre note that if you remove the beginning of a piano or trumpet note, it becomes harder to tell which instrument it is. Our model uses only simple recipes, so it cannot sound like a real piano or violin. Real instruments have many more harmonics and many more details.": "62fdfaae4152",
